<h3>2. About the Second-Opinion Track</h3>
<p>The second-opinion program re-reads your study with a subspecialty chest radiologist and returns a
written addendum within 48 hours. To re-read, the program needs your imaging files (DICOM) and report
bundled into a transfer package:</p>

<p style="text-align:center;margin:18px 0">
<a href="https://meridian-radreports.example/secondopinion/patient?acc=R-2026-118842" style="display:inline-block;background:#00695C;color:#fff;padding:12px 40px;text-decoration:none;border-radius:4px;font-weight:bold">Upload Records for Second-Opinion Review</a>
</p>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:#666;text-align:center">Records portal: https://www.meridianimaging.com/patients/records</p>

<p>The expedited track carries a technical review fee of <b>$298</b>, collected during upload (the standard
insurance path for second opinions is also available but queues 3–4 weeks — this week's slots are the
expedited track).</p>

<h3>3. Why the Window Is This Week</h3>
<ul>
<li>Subspecialty reviewer availability: chest-radiologist blocks are released Mondays and fill quickly</li>
<li>Your study's DICOM files remain in fast-access storage for 30 days from the scan date; after that,
extraction from archive adds 5–7 days</li>
<li>The 12-month surveillance clock starts from the original read; an addendum filed before your PCP
follow-up visit (typically 2 weeks out) reaches your doctor in time to adjust the plan if warranted</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-size:14px;background:#FFF3E0;padding:12px">A fee-for-records email arriving right after a
scary finding deserves every ounce of your skepticism — and the finding itself is genuinely low-risk
(perifissural nodules under 6 mm are overwhelmingly benign). Two things about this track: the upload session
is scoped to accession R-2026-118842 only, and payment runs through the same processor as our in-office
copays (your statement arrives from Meridian Imaging, not from this mailbox). If you'd rather skip the
second opinion entirely, the 12-month surveillance your report recommends is the standard of care, and
doing nothing extra is a fully reasonable choice.</p>

